{"name":"Baby Melon","hex":"#ffa468","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"baby","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very young human, particularly from birth to a couple of years old or until walking is fully mastered.\n\nA very young human, even if not yet born.","A very young human, even if not yet born."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Picked when small and immature (as in baby corn, baby potatoes).","Newest (overall, or in some group or state); most inexperienced."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/baby"},{"term":"melon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Some of the plants of the family Cucurbitaceae grown for food, generally not including the cucumber.\nGenus Cucumis, the true melon (including various cultigens like honeydew and cantaloupes), the horned melon, and others.\nGenus Citrullus, the watermelon and others.\nBenincasa hispida, the winter melon.\nMomordica charantia, the bitter melon.","Genus Cucumis, the true melon (including various cultigens like honeydew and cantaloupes), the horned melon, and others."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a light pinkish orange color, like that of melon flesh."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/melon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Infant","blocks":[{"text":"An infant is a very young human being. Baby is an alternative term for infant. The terms may also be used to refer to juvenile beings of other organisms. A newborn is, in mainstream use, an infant who is only hours, days, or weeks old; while in medical contexts, a newborn or neonate is an infant in the first 28 days after birth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/The_newborn_baby_in_men.jpg/330px-The_newborn_baby_in_men.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infant"},{"title":"Melon","blocks":[{"text":"A melon is any of various plants of the family Cucurbitaceae with sweet, edible, and fleshy fruit. It can also specifically refer to Cucumis melo, commonly known as the \"true melon\" or simply \"melon\". The term \"melon\" can apply to both the plant and its fruit. Botanically, a melon is a kind of berry, specifically a \"pepo\". The word melon derives from Latin melopepo, which is the latinization of the Greek μηλοπέπων (mēlopepōn), meaning \"melon\", itself a compound of μῆλον (mēlon), \"apple\", treefruit \" and πέπων (pepōn), amongst others \"a kind of gourd or melon\". Many different cultivars have been produced, particularly of the true melon, such as the cantaloupe and honeydew."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Cantaloupe_and_canary_melon.jpg/330px-Cantaloupe_and_canary_melon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melon"},{"title":"Shades of orange","blocks":[{"text":"In optics, orange has a wavelength between approximately 585 and 620 nm and a hue of 30° in HSV color space. In the RGB color space it is a secondary color numerically halfway between gamma-compressed red and yellow, as can be seen in the RGB color wheel. The complementary color of orange is azure. Orange pigments are largely in the ochre or cadmium families, and absorb mostly blue light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Shades_of_Orange.svg/330px-Shades_of_Orange.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_orange"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Views_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg/330px-Views_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Views_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg/330px-Views_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_co